From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: 2026-09-03
Subject: [PATCH 003/NNN] srslog: give the bundled fmt priority over a system one

bundled/fmt/format.cc includes "fmt/format-inl.h". That does not resolve
next to the source file, which lives in lib/src/srslog/bundled/fmt/, so
the quoted include falls through to the -I list. The directory holding
the bundled headers is added there without BEFORE, so it lands after the
include paths inherited from the top level.

On macOS those inherited paths contain /opt/homebrew/include, and if fmt
is installed through Homebrew the build silently compiles the tree's
format.cc against a different major version of the library. With fmt
12.2.0 present that produces around a hundred errors naming symbols that
the version in tree still expects: basic_data, float_specs, grouping_impl,
FMT_SNPRINTF, dynamic_arg_list.

Adding BEFORE puts the bundled directory first, so the tree compiles
against its own copy whatever else is installed. Nothing about this is
specific to Darwin; any system with fmt on an explicit include path was
exposed the same way.

--- a/lib/src/srslog/CMakeLists.txt	2026-09-03 15:05:02
+++ b/lib/src/srslog/CMakeLists.txt	2026-09-03 15:05:02
@@ -24,7 +24,12 @@
     srslog_c.cpp
     event_trace.cpp)
 
-include_directories(${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/lib/include/srsran/srslog/bundled/)
+# BEFORE matters. bundled/fmt/format.cc includes "fmt/format-inl.h", which
+# does not resolve next to the source file, so it falls through to the -I
+# list. Any fmt installed system wide, for example Homebrew's on macOS, is
+# reached first without this and supplies a different major version of the
+# library than the tree expects.
+include_directories(BEFORE ${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/lib/include/srsran/srslog/bundled/)
 include_directories(${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/lib/include/srsran/srslog/formatters)
 
 set(SOURCES
